\subsection{Source-Reachable and Path-Dependent Lyapunov Refinement}
\label{sec:stability-widening-source-path}

The envelope theorem of Sec.~\ref{sec:stability-widening-phase-a} is deliberately
strong: it allows every predictable switching sequence in the compact family
$\Pi^{\rm env}$, even if no execution of the estimator can realize that
sequence.  This robustness statement is retained unchanged.  For quantitative
certification of the deployed source, however, it is unnecessarily conservative
to combine arbitrary parameter jumps, pseudo-update phases, covariance states,
bias-enable states, and accepted-update patterns that are individually
admissible but mutually unreachable.  This subsection adds a second route that
uses only source-reachable words and permits transient Lyapunov growth inside a
certified word provided the complete word contracts.

\subsubsection{Source-reachable finite-window contraction}

For a starting sample $k$ and integer $N\ge1$, let
$\Pi^{\rm src}_{k,N}$ denote the compact family of length-$N$ proof sequences
that can be generated by the implemented estimator from the declared reachable
Live set.  Each sequence contains the same objects used elsewhere in the proof:
the held/active accelerometer-bias state, pseudo-measurement phase, magnetic
gauge state, accepted-update pattern, covariance recursion, staged or Live
OU/$R_S$ parameters, and all other source guards that determine the homogeneous
matrix.  By construction,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\Pi^{\rm src}_{k,N}\subseteq\Pi^{\rm env}_{k,N}.}
 \label{eq:widen-src-subset}
\end{equation}
Let $\widetilde\Psi(k+N,k;\pi)$ be the homogeneous dimensionless transition and
define
\begin{equation}
 \kappa_N^{\rm src}:=
 \sup_k\sup_{\pi\in\Pi^{\rm src}_{k,N}}
 \|\widetilde\Psi(k+N,k;\pi)\|,
 \qquad
 \kappa_N^{\rm env}:=
 \sup_k\sup_{\pi\in\Pi^{\rm env}_{k,N}}
 \|\widetilde\Psi(k+N,k;\pi)\|.
 \label{eq:widen-src-kappa}
\end{equation}
Then Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-subset} gives immediately
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\kappa_N^{\rm src}\le\kappa_N^{\rm env}.}
 \label{eq:widen-src-monotone}
\end{equation}
Thus the source-specific contraction test can pass even when a rigorous
arbitrary-envelope bound is inconclusive.

\begin{theorem}[Source-reachable finite-window UES]
\label{thm:widen-src-kappa-ues}
Assume the physical and covariance compactness hypotheses of
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-envelope-ues}.  If, for some finite $N$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\kappa_N^{\rm src}<1,}
 \label{eq:widen-src-kappa-condition}
\end{equation}
then the homogeneous Live recursion is uniformly exponentially stable over the
source-reachable family.  Individual samples inside the $N$-step window may
have norm gain greater than one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At lifted indices $k_j=k_0+jN$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-kappa-condition}
gives
$\|z_{k_{j+1}}\|\le\kappa_N^{\rm src}\|z_{k_j}\|$.  Iteration yields geometric
contraction at the lifted indices.  On the compact source family,
$\bar A_{\rm src}:=\sup\|\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}\|<\infty$, so the at most
$N-1$ intermediate samples are bounded by
$\bar A_{\rm src}^{N-1}$.  Combining the lifted contraction and finite prefix
bound gives UES at every sample.
\end{proof}

The theorem is an additive refinement, not a replacement for
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-envelope-ues}.  Whenever the arbitrary-envelope
certificate verifies $\kappa_N^{\rm env}<1$, the source certificate passes by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-monotone}.  The converse need not hold because the
envelope permits words that the source never generates.

\subsubsection{Finite source graph and path-dependent metrics}

A single Euclidean norm or a single common quadratic metric can still be
conservative because the source has distinct proof phases.  Introduce a finite
proof graph
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr G_{\rm src}=(\mathscr M,\mathscr E)
 \label{eq:widen-src-graph}
\end{equation}
whose nodes cover the compact source-reachable proof state.  A node may encode,
for example, the held/active bias state, pseudo-update phase, magnetic state,
and a declared compact cell of covariance and scheduled parameters.  Each edge
$e=(i,j)\in\mathscr E$ carries the compact matrix family
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr A_e:=\left\{\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}:
 \text{the source can move from node $i$ to node $j$ in one sample}\right\}.
 \label{eq:widen-src-edge-family}
\end{equation}
The graph is an outer cover of the source, not of $\Pi^{\rm env}$: every actual
source transition must lie on an edge, while edges known to be unreachable are
excluded.  Continuous covariance and tuning values remain inside the compact
edge families and are not discretized as point samples.

For a graph word
$w=(i_0,e_0,i_1,\ldots,e_{m-1},i_m)$, let
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr P_w:=\left\{
 \mat\Phi_w=\mat A_{m-1}\cdots\mat A_0:
 \mat A_r\in\mathscr A_{e_r}
 \text{ and the complete tuple is source-reachable}\right\}.
 \label{eq:widen-src-word-family}
\end{equation}
Retaining complete source reachability in $\mathscr P_w$ prevents another
independent-supremum relaxation between successive edges.

Let $\mathscr W_{\rm src}$ be a finite collection of such words with lengths
$m(w)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 1\le m_-\le m(w)\le m_+<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-src-word-length}
\end{equation}
Call $\mathscr W_{\rm src}$ \emph{source complete} if every infinite admissible
source path can be tiled successively by words from $\mathscr W_{\rm src}$.
The tiling need not be periodic and different words may have different lengths.

Assign a symmetric matrix $\mat P_i\succ0$ to each graph node.  Define uniform
metric bounds
\begin{equation}
 p_-\mat I\preceq\mat P_i\preceq p_+\mat I,
 \qquad i\in\mathscr M.
 \label{eq:widen-path-P-bounds}
\end{equation}
For every certified word require
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mat\Phi_w^\top\mat P_{i_m}\mat\Phi_w-\mat P_{i_0}
 \preceq-\mat Q_w,
 \qquad
 \mat Q_w\succeq q_-\mat I,
 \quad q_->0,}
 \label{eq:widen-path-lmi}
\end{equation}
for every $\mat\Phi_w\in\mathscr P_w$.  This is a robust finite-word matrix
inequality.  A computer-assisted verifier must enclose the complete compact
matrix family; checking finitely many nominal trajectories alone is not a proof.

\begin{theorem}[Source-complete path Lyapunov UES]
\label{thm:widen-path-ues}
Suppose $\mathscr W_{\rm src}$ is source complete,
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-P-bounds}--\eqref{eq:widen-path-lmi} hold, and the
one-step source family is bounded.  Then the homogeneous Live recursion is UES
on every source-reachable execution.  At certified word boundaries,
\begin{equation}
 V^+\le\lambda_{\mathscr W}V^-,
 \qquad
 \lambda_{\mathscr W}:=1-\frac{q_-}{p_+}<1,
 \label{eq:widen-path-contraction}
\end{equation}
with $V=z^\top P_i z$.  No one-step decrease is required inside a word.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For a word beginning in node $i_0$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-lmi} gives
\begin{align}
 V^+-V^-
 &\le-q_-\|z^-\|^2
 \le-\frac{q_-}{p_+}V^- ,
\end{align}
which proves Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction}.  Source completeness tiles
every infinite source execution by contracting words of uniformly bounded
length.  The compact one-step matrix family gives a finite amplification bound
for the at most $m_+-1$ samples between word boundaries.  Uniform metric
bounds then convert the word-boundary estimate to a sample-by-sample UES
estimate.
\end{proof}

If desired, replace $1-q_-/p_+$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction} by any verified nonnegative upper bound on
the generalized word gain
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_w=
 \sup_{\Phi_w\in\mathscr P_w}
 \lambda_{\max}\!\left(
 P_{i_0}^{-1/2}\Phi_w^\top P_{i_m}\Phi_wP_{i_0}^{-1/2}
 \right)<1.
 \label{eq:widen-path-generalized-gain}
\end{equation}
This avoids converting every path to a common $q_-$ when separate path margins
are available.

\begin{remark}[Why the path metric can be strictly less conservative]
\label{rem:widen-path-dominance}
The Phase-B Euclidean $N$-step certificate is contained as the special choice
$P_i=I$ with fixed word length $N$.  A common quadratic metric is contained as
the special choice $P_i=P$ for every node.  The path certificate admits both
$P_i\ne P_j$ and multi-sample words, so a prediction or weak-update sample may
increase one metric while the complete source word decreases the appropriate
endpoint metric.  Therefore failure of a common one-step or arbitrary-envelope
certificate does not imply failure of
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-path-ues}.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{Constructive constants without the $\kappa_N\!\to M$ relaxation}

Let
\begin{equation}
 B_{\rm pre}:=
 \sup_{w\in\mathscr W_{\rm src}}
 \sup_{\substack{0\le r<m(w)\\
                  \text{source-reachable prefixes}}}
 \left\|
 P_{i_r}^{1/2}\Phi_{w,r}P_{i_0}^{-1/2}
 \right\|,
 \label{eq:widen-path-prefix}
\end{equation}
where $\Phi_{w,r}$ is the length-$r$ homogeneous prefix and the node index at
that prefix is $i_r$.  Compactness and the finite maximum word length imply
$B_{\rm pre}<\infty$.  Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-contraction} and
\eqref{eq:widen-path-prefix} gives directly
\begin{equation}
 \|z_k\|
 \le
 \sqrt{\frac{p_+}{p_-}}\,B_{\rm pre}
 \lambda_{\mathscr W}^{\,n(k)/2}\|z_{k_0}\|,
 \label{eq:widen-path-ues-bound}
\end{equation}
where $n(k)$ is the number of completed certified words.  Since every word has
length at most $m_+$,
$n(k)\ge\lfloor(k-k_0)/m_+\rfloor-1$.  Hence
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-ues-bound} supplies explicit UES constants directly
from $(P_i,\lambda_w,B_{\rm pre},m_+)$ and does not require the conservative
intermediate chain
$\kappa_N\rightarrow\rho\rightarrow M\rightarrow p_+$ of the broad-envelope
construction.

\subsubsection{Lifted nonlinear direct-decrease certificate}

The same source words can be used in the nonlinear theorem.  For a word
$w\in\mathscr W_{\rm src}$ let
\begin{equation}
 z^+=\mathcal F_w(z,0)=\Phi_w z+\eta_w(z)
 \label{eq:widen-path-nonlinear-lift}
\end{equation}
be the exact zero-input lifted error map obtained by composing the implemented
prediction, all accepted measurement corrections, full $S=0$ cross-gain,
quaternion injection/reset, covariance recursion, and source mode transitions
along that word.  The accumulated nonlinear term $\eta_w$ is defined by the
identity in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-path-nonlinear-lift}; it is not formed by
independently maximizing one-step remainder norms.

For compact source-node tubes $\mathcal T_i$ whose complete sample prefixes are
safe, define
\begin{equation}
 \mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}:=
 \inf_{w\in\mathscr W_{\rm src}}
 \inf_{\substack{z\in\mathcal T_{i_0}\\z\ne0\\
                  \text{all prefixes safe}}}
 \frac{
 z^\top P_{i_0}z-
 \mathcal F_w(z,0)^\top P_{i_m}\mathcal F_w(z,0)}
 {\|z\|^2}.
 \label{eq:widen-path-direct-margin}
\end{equation}
All continuous covariance, geometry, gain, and parameter values inside the word
are restricted to the same source-reachable compact family used in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-src-word-family}.

\begin{theorem}[Source-word direct-decrease regional practical ISS]
\label{thm:widen-path-direct-iss}
Assume a source-complete word family with safe prefixes and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-path-direct-condition}
\end{equation}
Then sufficiently small endpoint Lyapunov sublevels contained in the node tubes
are robustly invariant at certified word boundaries for sufficiently small
bounded deterministic input.  All intermediate samples remain in the declared
safe domain, and the resulting source execution satisfies a practical-ISS
estimate.  Positive one-step Lyapunov increments are permitted inside a word.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-path-direct-condition} gives a strict exact zero-input
decrease over every admissible source word.  On the compact prefix-safe lifted
family, the disturbance-to-endpoint map is uniformly bounded and locally
Lipschitz in the disturbance.  Young inequalities therefore preserve a strict
word-level inward margin for a sufficiently small disturbance budget.  Iteration
over the source-complete tiling gives practical ISS at word boundaries, while
the finite prefix bound gives the sample-by-sample estimate and safety.
\end{proof}

The one-step direct theorem of
Sec.~\ref{sec:stability-widening-structured} is recovered when every certified
word has length one and the node metric equals the original $P_k$.  Therefore
the source-word condition is an additive widening route: any trajectory that
passes the one-step direct test passes an appropriate length-one source-word
test, while a source word may still pass when some individual samples have
positive $\Delta V$.

\subsubsection{Verification contract and scope}

A rigorous source-path certificate reports at least
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \{\mathscr G_{\rm src},\mathscr W_{\rm src},
 P_i,p_-,p_+,\lambda_w,B_{\rm pre},m_+,
 \mu_{\Delta V}^{\rm path}\}.}
 \label{eq:widen-path-contract}
\end{equation}
Its deterministic pass conditions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.7em]
 \item every reachable source transition is covered by
       $\mathscr G_{\rm src}$;
 \item $\mathscr W_{\rm src}$ is source complete;
 \item every robust word matrix inequality or equivalent generalized-gain bound
       is verified over the complete continuous source-reachable family;
 \item all nonlinear word prefixes remain in their declared safe tubes; and
 \item the selected linear or nonlinear word margin is strict.
\end{enumerate}
Interval arithmetic, affine arithmetic, Taylor models, branch-and-bound, robust
LMIs over certified matrix enclosures, or another validated enclosure method may
be used.  Monte Carlo trajectories and dense sampling are diagnostics only and
cannot establish the suprema or infima above.

This refinement changes neither the estimator nor the adaptation law.  It does
not weaken the arbitrary-envelope theorem and it does not assume that the tuner
is stability critical.  Rather, it uses source reachability only to obtain a
less conservative quantitative certificate for the deployed implementation.
